xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">ngur smrig pa chen po</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">ngur dmigs pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rong gi dge bshes ngur dmig pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">brang ti dar ma snying po</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">brang ti dar snying</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">'brang</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">ngur mig</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">'ur smrig</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">brang ti</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rdu</persName><birth notAfter="1085-12-31">before 1085</birth><death notBefore="1105-01-01">after 1105</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="BDRC">https://purl.bdrc.io/resource/P10011</idno><idno type="APIS">4291</idno><note type="comment">We follow Stearns ([RA3FK3BK]: 245, n. 173 and n. 176) in assuming that Ngur smrig pa is the same person as Brang ti Dar ma snying po, a foremost figure in the transmission of the Abhidharma in Tibet.&#13;
